  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "AutoMapperLite 4.4.1 — current official release; all prior versions are deprecated.\n\n\t\t\t• Fixed: a ForPath mapping targeting a leaf property that legitimately resolves to\n\t\t\t  null (e.g. an optional string) either lost that null to a freshly-constructed\n\t\t\t  instance, or — for a leaf type with no public parameterless constructor reachable\n\t\t\t  via reflection, most commonly string itself — threw InvalidOperationException\n\t\t\t  outright. The nested-path walk tried to descend one segment past the actual leaf;\n\t\t\t  only intermediate segments need that.\n\t\t\t• Added: same-name nested collection destination *properties* now also accept an\n\t\t\t  array (T[]) destination, matching the array support the top-level\n\t\t\t  Map<TDestination>(object) API already had since 4.4.0. Previously an\n\t\t\t  array-typed nested collection property was silently left unmapped even when a map\n\t\t\t  was registered for the item types; it is now populated the same way a List<T>-\n\t\t\t  or interface-typed nested collection property already was. Purely additive — no\n\t\t\t  existing mapping output changes, since this only fills in a property that was\n\t\t\t  previously always left at its default.\n\t\t\t• Documentation: corrected several stale claims left over from before the 4.4.0\n\t\t\t  mapping-engine redesign — destination construction is described as a compiled\n\t\t\t  constructor call (Expression.New) rather than Activator.CreateInstance in the\n\t\t\t  guide/FAQ, the home page's comparison FAQ answer no longer claims AutoMapperLite\n\t\t\t  trails AutoMapper on collections (it has beaten AutoMapper there since 4.4.0), and\n\t\t\t  SECURITY.md's supported-version table no longer references 4.2.x, a version that\n\t\t\t  was never published to NuGet (see CHANGELOG.md/CLAUDE.md for the verified publish\n\t\t\t  history), and the performance page no longer overstates Activator.CreateInstance's\n\t\t\t  elimination (it still backs the interpreted ForPath walk and the untyped\n\t\t\t  collection-dispatch fallback, just not the compiled per-property path). No\n\t\t\t  code-behavior changes from the documentation fixes.\n\t\t\t• No public API breaking changes since 4.0.2.\n\n\t\t\tPrevious (4.4.0): mapping engine and performance redesign — each source/destination\n\t\t\ttype pair now compiles into a cached delegate via System.Linq.Expressions on first\n\t\t\tuse instead of being interpreted through reflection; added\n\t\t\tIMapper.Map<TSource,TDestination>(TSource) and broader collection-destination\n\t\t\tsupport (T[], IList<T>, ICollection<T>, IReadOnlyList<T>,\n\t\t\tIReadOnlyCollection<T>, IEnumerable<T>); fixed a collection-loop\n\t\t\tinterface-dispatch cost, flipping AutoMapperLite from trailing AutoMapper on\n\t\t\tcollection mapping to beating it at every size from 1,000 items upward. See\n\t\t\tdocs/performance.html and docs/changelog.html for full measured results and history.",
